WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.908 --> 00:00:03.339
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.880
My name is Grant
Fleming, and I'm

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:11.580
a data scientist at
Elder Research where

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.430
I work on data science
consulting projects

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:16.890
with various public
sector clients.

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:20.400
And in that work, along
with a lot of the work

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.070
that I'm doing
outside of work work,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.310
I'm having to wrestle with
the issue of fairness and data

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:29.910
science, specifically things
like, how do we make sure

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:32.850
that the predictions being
generated by our models

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:33.810
are fair?

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.480
How do we make
sure we're actually

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.940
recording the correct
information our data

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.640
and not leaving certain
important factors out?

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.470
And if we suspect that something
may have snuck in our model,

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:49.830
how do we make sure that
we're able to detect

00:00:49.830 --> 00:00:52.620
that element before we deploy
our model and cause harm

00:00:52.620 --> 00:00:54.210
intentionally.

00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.890
Because as we've
seen probably in some

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.900
of the news over
the past few years

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:00.960
this is an issue that's, if
not become more widespread,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.820
become more widely known.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:06.180
For example in
2018, Joy Buolamwini

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.780
and her research team at MIT
published the gender shades

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.260
work that they did on facial
recognition algorithms

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.980
from companies like
Amazon, IBM, and others.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.690
And in this work, they found out
that these facial recognition

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:25.410
facial recognition
algorithms were actually

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.860
having much higher error rates
detecting darker skinned faces

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:30.840
versus lighter skinned faces.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.410
And that obviously is
something that we would not

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:36.990
want to see in any
sort of fair model.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:39.810
Similarly, we saw
something similar in 2019

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.530
in the health care sector
where an algorithm furnished

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:45.660
to hospitals throughout
the US, which

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:51.940
was tasked with helping to
allocate treatment to patients

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.650
within the hospitals was
systematically underestimating

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:59.110
the health care needs
for black patients

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.330
relative to white
patients or patients

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.400
from other ethnic groups.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:06.700
Again, likely a case
of unintended bias,

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:11.528
but nonetheless something that
led to harms in the real world.

00:02:11.528 --> 00:02:13.070
And I could spend
[INAUDIBLE] talking

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:16.790
about these issues across the
various different examples

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:20.070
which I'm not including,
but that's not productive.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:21.860
Maybe what's more
productive, then, is

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:26.010
coming to an understanding of
why this is actually happening.

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:29.200
So from my perspective
at least, it

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.430
seems like this is in
large part the result of us

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:36.340
as data scientists thinking
that our tools alone--

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:38.290
the technical tools,
the code, and the data

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:41.350
and the models that
we are able to build--

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.680
are enough to solve these
complex social problems.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:47.170
Like duct tape, we
use them whenever

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.420
we can to fix whatever we can.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:53.050
And some situations are
helped by duct tape,

00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.620
but duct tape cannot
alone solve everything.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:58.120
I think that's a
good metaphor here

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:00.280
for how we should
think about it,

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:02.540
or maybe how we
do think about it.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.220
How we should think about
it instead is our toolkit

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:09.940
is not duct tape that should
be used wildly wherever we can,

00:03:09.940 --> 00:03:13.540
but rather our toolkit is
one that, along with people

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:18.160
who have the subject matter and
lived expertise in the areas

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.820
that we seem to operate
in, they along with us

00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:25.990
can lay a brick by brick
a solution to a problem,

00:03:25.990 --> 00:03:28.390
or at least get
closer to solving it.

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.650
And this is twofold.

00:03:30.650 --> 00:03:33.520
We from them can get
a greater appreciation

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.750
for the factors that can't be
encoded in our data or

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.880
aren't encoded in our data,
but are nonetheless

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:43.240
relevant to our work,
and they in turn

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:47.710
can help us to better diagnose
issues within our models

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.110
before they're deployed.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:50.710
Sounds good.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.460
But how do we actually do this?

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.100
How do we actually
do responsible,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.630
ethical, trustworthy,
however you

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.420
want to call it, data
science in practice.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:00.160
It's the million
dollar question,

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.820
and a number of researchers are
working to try and answer this.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.220
And to my reading,
it seems like we've

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.010
coalesced around the
three main factors that

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.230
go into making a
data science project,

00:04:11.230 --> 00:04:14.740
we'll just say responsible
data science project.

00:04:14.740 --> 00:04:18.940
Those being, first,
fairness in procedures.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.310
So basically, was the project
designed in such a way

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.890
that it can be conducted fairly?

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.410
Did it include a
diverse range of voices

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.840
and all relevant stakeholders?

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:32.360
Next would be fairness
in predictions.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:34.810
So when the model
is actually built,

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:37.330
does the model
perform equally well

00:04:37.330 --> 00:04:39.640
across different
groups within the data?

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:42.680
And the final one being
fairness in outcomes.

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.790
So once the actual
process of the model

00:04:45.790 --> 00:04:49.450
is sensed down and
is more or less fair

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:54.640
and even if we get relative
fairness in our predictions,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:57.280
does the impact of
those predictions

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:00.100
actually improve fairness
in the real world?

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:04.170
Are the impacts of those
predictions equitable?

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:05.670
So fairness and
outcomes obviously

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.688
is something that's a little
bit harder to measure maybe

00:05:08.688 --> 00:05:09.480
than the other two.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:11.670
But we'll attempt
to talk about it

00:05:11.670 --> 00:05:15.010
at least a little bit over the
course of this presentation.

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:17.440
And the way we'll
move forward with this

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:21.715
is going through each of the
three of these in more detail,

00:05:21.715 --> 00:05:25.420
and we'll go through this in the
context of a practical example.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.760
But hopefully by
maintaining all three

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:31.690
of these within our projects,
if not doing our projects

00:05:31.690 --> 00:05:34.120
in a way that is
perfectly fair, we

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:39.200
will get closer to a fair
responsible data science.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.840
So let's begin with
fairness in procedures.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.720
And where better to
start thinking about that

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.030
than ensuring inclusion?

00:05:49.030 --> 00:05:52.960
If you don't have a diverse
perspective or diverse group

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:56.590
of perspectives on your project,
in all likelihood something

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.890
could slip through that would
cause an unintended harm when

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.360
your model is deployed.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.430
This is especially relevant
for someone like me

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:05.950
who not only is a
consultant who's

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:10.210
often coming into a data science
project in a subject matter

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.610
context where I have
less expertise than maybe

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.720
some of the people
I'm working with,

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:20.590
but also because I'm a
white man and that comes--

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:25.060
that carries with it
certain biases--some of them

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:28.420
benign, some of them harmful--
in how I see the world

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.070
and operate within it.

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:33.910
And it's important
for me and the people

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:35.710
I work with on projects
and the clients

00:06:35.710 --> 00:06:39.520
I work with that those biases
are checked by the views

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:42.200
that other people can
bring to the table.

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.620
And I think critically,
this is something

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:49.870
that is worthy in all projects
besides being the right thing

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.290
to do.

00:06:51.290 --> 00:06:54.150
Next though would be
reproducible code.

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:58.650
We don't want code that just
runs on our own machines.

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:01.110
This is maybe easier
said than done,

00:07:01.110 --> 00:07:04.080
but there are fortunately
within R ecosystem

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:06.480
a number of packages
available for this.

00:07:06.480 --> 00:07:08.880
I don't go too much into
detail on what those

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:12.150
are, because so many
other people have already

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.860
spoken in depth about these.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:16.030
I'll reference two of them here.

00:07:16.030 --> 00:07:19.620
The first would probably
be Dr. Anna Krystalli.

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.170
She has a great
talk called "Putting

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:24.360
the R into reproducible
research", which

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:25.650
you can find by googling.

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.120
It should come up.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.250
And then another
person Karthik Ram

00:07:29.250 --> 00:07:33.000
delivered a talk at the previous
RStudio conference in 2019.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:33.570
Google that.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:34.740
You should find it.

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:37.650
I don't remember the title,
but between the two of them,

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:40.080
they covered a lot of
really great materials

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:43.330
for doing reproducible
work within R.

00:07:43.330 --> 00:07:45.950
But back to fairness
and procedures, finally

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:48.460
to wrap this all up,
we want to make sure

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:52.540
that we document everything we
do and every decision we make.

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.540
In the same way that we want to
have our code be reproducible

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:59.140
by technical people, we also
want nontechnical people

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:02.320
who may not be able to actually
access our code directly

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.870
to be able to pore
through our results

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:06.760
in a way that's easy
for them to interpret,

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:09.150
and that they can
actually evaluate.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:12.720
And the Google Brain
ethical AI team

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:14.760
has done a lot of
good work on this.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:17.700
They've come up with
data sheets, model cards,

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:19.390
and audit reports.

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:23.280
So three different sort of
documentation modalities

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:26.070
that you can all think
of is nutrition labels,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:28.680
I guess, for each
part of the project.

00:08:28.680 --> 00:08:31.470
And even though we
don't yet have any way

00:08:31.470 --> 00:08:34.530
to do these within R,
hopefully soon there

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:37.110
will be some sort of
R markdown templates

00:08:37.110 --> 00:08:39.273
that make this easier for us.

00:08:39.273 --> 00:08:41.190
I'm certainly on my own
end trying to put together

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:42.732
a package for it,
and we'll hopefully

00:08:42.732 --> 00:08:44.933
have some more updates
on that in the future.

00:08:44.933 --> 00:08:46.350
Point being though
if anyone wants

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:48.390
to look at good
examples for these,

00:08:48.390 --> 00:08:52.700
they can Google the research
done by Timnit Gebru, Deborah

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:55.350
Raji, and Megan Mitchell.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.120
And for good examples
of model cards,

00:08:57.120 --> 00:08:59.130
I specifically
definitely recommend

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:02.070
looking up the TensorFlow
model card toolkit.

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:06.000
So TensorFlow already has built
in functionality for building

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.298
model cards, and they
have some good examples

00:09:08.298 --> 00:09:09.840
for that there are
things that we may

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:12.950
want to track in our own work.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:17.180
With that being done, you
have a relatively fair process

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:18.150
in place.

00:09:18.150 --> 00:09:20.240
And so you're in good
shape to start now

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.790
thinking about fairness
and predictions.

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.985
Starting with:
predicting accurately.

00:09:25.985 --> 00:09:27.860
This doesn't need a
whole lot of development,

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:30.980
but I include this here
to make sure to point out

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:34.880
that if you do not have
high predictive performance

00:09:34.880 --> 00:09:38.000
overall in your model, by no
conception can it be fair.

00:09:38.000 --> 00:09:40.910
Even if you perform
equally across groups,

00:09:40.910 --> 00:09:44.750
getting a 10% level of
performance across all of them

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:45.860
is meaningless.

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:48.710
And that's going to do you
and the people this model

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:52.120
is hoping to serve
no good whatsoever.

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.450
But more subtle and probably
more relevant to the work we do

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:57.740
is predicting equally.

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:00.920
And to explore this
in practical detail

00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:03.895
and how we can actually work
on this at own projects, we're

00:10:03.895 --> 00:10:08.760
going to go through an example with
the COMPAS Recidivism dataset.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:12.230
So this is a dataset released
by the public interest group

00:10:12.230 --> 00:10:17.330
ProPublica, and what it
tracks is demographic

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:21.740
and criminal records
information of defendants.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:24.860
And the goal of this is to
take all this information

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:27.320
and use it to predict
whether these defendants will

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.350
commit another crime
in the next two years.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:33.320
Because of the demographic
information within this data,

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:35.840
it's often used as
sort of the iris

00:10:35.840 --> 00:10:39.290
or Titanic dataset
for fairness methods

00:10:39.290 --> 00:10:42.320
and evaluation of fairness
maintaining approaches

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:43.820
within data science.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:48.530
As we're about to do here,
and as I already did here. So

00:10:48.530 --> 00:10:51.080
on this plot we can
see two different bars,

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:54.590
one for the accuracy of
a logistic regression

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:57.720
and one for the accuracy
of a random forest model.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:59.960
And again, these are taking
demographic and criminal

00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:03.170
history features and
predicting a binary yes/no,

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.450
will commit a crime,
will not commit a crime

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:07.430
within two years.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:11.680
We can see that they perform
roughly the same, nothing

00:11:11.680 --> 00:11:14.380
really jumping out here,
which is why maybe we

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:16.620
want to dig a little deeper.

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:19.270
Maybe we can look at
per-group metrics.

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:22.830
So in this case,
instead of just looking

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:24.690
at overall model
performance, we'll

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:29.030
still look at the performance
of one model versus the other.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:30.780
But we're going to
look at it with respect

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:33.370
to a protected feature
or sensitive feature,

00:11:33.370 --> 00:11:37.140
in this case, race, and then
the protected groups a.k.a.

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:40.530
the levels of that feature which
in this case would be Hispanic,

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:43.513
Other, Caucasian,
or African-American.

00:11:43.513 --> 00:11:45.180
And already here we
can see that there's

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:48.030
some differences or
disparities in performance

00:11:48.030 --> 00:11:49.230
coming to the fore.

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:53.520
With African-Americans
specifically the model

00:11:53.520 --> 00:11:56.490
getting the lowest accuracy
for African-Americans

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.810
relative to other groups.

00:11:58.810 --> 00:12:02.470
This is already a pretty
compelling result I think.

00:12:02.470 --> 00:12:05.760
It goes to show that overall
metrics alone are not

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.400
enough to be able
to identify when

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:12.360
you may have some issues of
unfairness in your model.

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:13.860
But we still don't
necessarily know

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:15.630
why these disparities exist.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.290
These disparities have to be
in part due to different rates

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:19.812
of error.

00:12:19.812 --> 00:12:22.020
And we don't know whether
that's more false positives

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.790
or false negatives for
the different groups.

00:12:24.790 --> 00:12:28.540
So let's actually look at
plots of error direction.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:33.130
And here is where we start to
see the real compelling causes

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:35.260
come to the fore here.

00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:40.030
With, in this case, the model hitting
a almost four times higher

00:12:40.030 --> 00:12:42.520
false positive rate error
for African-Americans

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:45.610
and Caucasians, and
similarly high rates

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:49.290
for other non-Caucasian groups.

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:51.060
This is clearly
an issue of bias.

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:54.210
And if we were not to rectify
this or communicate this

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:58.560
to our clients or the
non-technical people

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:00.000
we're working with
in this project

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.920
and we were to just
put a stamp on this

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.030
and let it go, in
the real world,

00:13:04.030 --> 00:13:07.230
this would perpetuate
all sorts of biases

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:08.250
that would be harmful.

00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:12.420
And we don't want that.

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:15.830
So to get a better context
to that before we actually

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:17.690
communicate those
conclusions, maybe we

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:19.310
want to be able
to say, OK, we've

00:13:19.310 --> 00:13:22.340
made every comparison between
every different groups

00:13:22.340 --> 00:13:24.740
of Caucasians with
African-Americans, Caucasians

00:13:24.740 --> 00:13:27.740
with Hispanics, and maybe
we care specifically

00:13:27.740 --> 00:13:29.110
about the relative performance.

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.400
So we want to look
at the differences.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.110
That's where fairness
metrics come in.

00:13:33.110 --> 00:13:34.790
They don't measure
absolute performance.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:36.620
They measure relative.

00:13:36.620 --> 00:13:38.450
When we look at it
on this plot here,

00:13:38.450 --> 00:13:41.690
it's still the same y-axis,
still the same x-axis,

00:13:41.690 --> 00:13:45.260
but now we get the percentages
of false positive rates

00:13:45.260 --> 00:13:49.490
for each group relative to the
privileged group Caucasians.

00:13:49.490 --> 00:13:51.290
And again, we can
see here that there's

00:13:51.290 --> 00:13:54.680
quite a lot of disparity.

00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:55.980
Why though?

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.970
What is it in the
features that are

00:13:58.970 --> 00:14:00.920
going into the model,
that are causing

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:03.050
these disparities to exist?

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:06.470
It gets hard to get at causality
at any point within data

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:10.310
science, but if we want to at
least understand more about how

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:13.830
the features impact these
predictions differently,

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:16.740
we don't want to look at
interpretability methods.

00:14:16.740 --> 00:14:20.120
And these are often explained
in the context of the black box,

00:14:20.120 --> 00:14:22.340
hence the image on the right.

00:14:22.340 --> 00:14:26.262
What they allow us to do
is open this black box,

00:14:26.262 --> 00:14:28.220
because a lot of these
interpretability methods

00:14:28.220 --> 00:14:31.520
are model agnostic, and they
can be used even on models

00:14:31.520 --> 00:14:34.430
like XGBoost or random
forest to generate

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:38.120
the sort of explanations maybe
not quite to the same level

00:14:38.120 --> 00:14:41.060
fidelity, but
approximately that we

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:44.480
would get from a linear model
like a linear regression

00:14:44.480 --> 00:14:47.020
or something like
logistic regression.

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:48.880
I'm not going to go
through these in detail

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:51.340
within this presentation
because the visuals for them

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:53.895
are a little complex, and
we don't have a lot of time.

00:14:53.895 --> 00:14:55.270
But I will mention
that they come

00:14:55.270 --> 00:14:58.360
in two main varieties, the
first being global methods, which

00:14:58.360 --> 00:15:00.835
explain the average
effective features globally.

00:15:00.835 --> 00:15:02.710
You can think of this
as like the coefficient

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:04.220
of a regression.

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:06.100
And if you want to
read more about them,

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.870
you can look up something like
permutation feature importance,

00:15:08.870 --> 00:15:11.470
which ought to be well
known to a number of people,

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:14.560
partial dependence plots,
or individual conditional

00:15:14.560 --> 00:15:17.120
expectation plots.

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:20.360
We can also look at
something in the category

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.280
of the local methods,
and this could

00:15:22.280 --> 00:15:28.100
be say LIME or Shapley values or
derivatives of either of those.

00:15:28.100 --> 00:15:29.990
And these are really
powerful because they

00:15:29.990 --> 00:15:33.710
allow us to say for an
individual prediction on one

00:15:33.710 --> 00:15:35.700
observation,

00:15:35.700 --> 00:15:39.300
each feature value we can
quantify the exact amount

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:41.140
that it contributed
into the prediction.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:42.630
So if someone were
to ask, hey, why

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:45.450
did I get this specific
classification from your model?

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:47.850
You can tell them this
feature contributed this much,

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:50.560
this contributed
this much, et cetera.

00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:53.430
A lot of useful
explanation there.

00:15:53.430 --> 00:15:55.890
Hopefully this is something
that we can end up

00:15:55.890 --> 00:15:58.210
applying to most of our
models, and it's certainly

00:15:58.210 --> 00:16:01.620
a set of techniques which are
well developed within the R package

00:16:01.620 --> 00:16:03.150
ecosystem currently.

00:16:03.150 --> 00:16:06.990
The IML package by Christoph
Molnar is available.

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:09.630
And I use it in my work,
and I think it's fantastic.

00:16:09.630 --> 00:16:12.630
But there's also
the vip and DALEX--

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:16.890
D-A-L-E-X I believe--packages
which include a number

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:20.040
of interpretability
methods as well.

00:16:20.040 --> 00:16:24.420
And that all about wraps up the
fairness and prediction side.

00:16:24.420 --> 00:16:27.290
So again, if you're
able in your own work

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:30.150
to ensure those three
main points are hit,

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:34.570
then you're well on your way to
having more fair predictions,

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:37.060
which then leads us
to the thorny issue

00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:42.190
and possible future-looking
set of solutions for fairness

00:16:42.190 --> 00:16:43.610
in outcomes.

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:44.980
And why is this future--

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:46.120
why is this future leaning?

00:16:46.120 --> 00:16:47.920
Why is this a thorny
set of issues?

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:53.090
Well, outcomes are all
about the consequences

00:16:53.090 --> 00:16:57.050
and how we consider fairness in
the context of the real world.

00:16:57.050 --> 00:16:59.280
Because these outcomes
and the impacts

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:01.730
that they have on people are
not necessarily something

00:17:01.730 --> 00:17:04.339
that we can measure in
the data or at least can--

00:17:04.339 --> 00:17:07.849
or at least do measure in the
data, it's something that

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:12.500
requires conversations, not
just with the people who

00:17:12.500 --> 00:17:14.960
may be we're working with
who are non-technical

00:17:14.960 --> 00:17:17.720
who have the subject matter
expertise for the projects

00:17:17.720 --> 00:17:21.950
we're working on, but also the
people impacted by the models.

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:24.650
And talking with all
these different groups

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:27.440
and coming up with some
conception of fairness

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:32.270
to say, OK, maybe
we're going to optimize

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:35.977
for certain predictions despite
these predictions coming

00:17:35.977 --> 00:17:38.060
at a loss of predictive
performance for our model,

00:17:38.060 --> 00:17:41.480
because ultimately that's
the equitable thing to do.

00:17:41.480 --> 00:17:43.670
That's something that's
very project specific.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.610
It doesn't make a lot of
sense for me or anyone else

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:51.320
to give any sort of real
definitive guidance on that.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:54.140
And just for a little
bit more detail, maybe

00:17:54.140 --> 00:17:58.580
an example to make this clear,
you can think of fairness

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:01.040
in outcomes as being relevant
in a case where maybe we're

00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:04.460
granting, deciding whether
someone deserves a scholarship,

00:18:04.460 --> 00:18:06.230
binary yes/no.

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:09.860
A yes would benefit
someone who comes

00:18:09.860 --> 00:18:12.500
from maybe a more disadvantaged
background much more

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:15.490
than someone who comes from
a more privileged background.

00:18:15.490 --> 00:18:17.240
And again that's not
necessarily something

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:19.390
we measure within our model.

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:21.840
But if it's something we
want to try and work towards,

00:18:21.840 --> 00:18:24.300
then we need to make
sure to mitigate the bias

00:18:24.300 --> 00:18:29.020
that we see in our model in a
specific way to serve that end.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:31.980
And that's where bias
mitigation techniques come in.

00:18:31.980 --> 00:18:35.430
And I have the star on word
there from the previous slide

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:38.100
because these techniques,
they sometimes work well,

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:39.030
they sometimes don't.

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:42.240
But by and large they
all have tradeoffs.

00:18:42.240 --> 00:18:45.432
And that's because you're
either modifying the data,

00:18:45.432 --> 00:18:46.890
in the case of
pre-processing, that

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:50.640
goes into the model
itself, or the predictions

00:18:50.640 --> 00:18:54.450
that come out of the model to
reach some notion of fairness.

00:18:54.450 --> 00:18:58.260
And while that may improve that
specific notion of fairness,

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:00.570
you may see that
there are losses

00:19:00.570 --> 00:19:02.160
and predictive
performance or fairness

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:04.183
across other dimensions.

00:19:04.183 --> 00:19:05.850
And to show this,
we'll go back and look

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:07.660
at the COMPAS example.

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:10.320
So here, I have
a plot again that

00:19:10.320 --> 00:19:14.520
shows protected groups on the
y-axis and false positive rate

00:19:14.520 --> 00:19:17.070
on the x-axis.

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:20.000
But now we're looking instead
of at logistic regression

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:23.220
and random forest, we're looking
at just the random forest.

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:25.740
But we're looking at two
sets of predictions here.

00:19:25.740 --> 00:19:31.920
One where the prediction
thresholds are all set at 0.54.

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:34.920
If you have a higher
than 0.5, will

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:38.460
predict that you will commit
a crime, lower the opposite.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:42.960
In one it's set to an optimal
level for each class such

00:19:42.960 --> 00:19:45.730
that it minimizes
false positive rate.

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:47.550
And we can see here
that it does that well.

00:19:47.550 --> 00:19:49.830
All groups actually
have a large decrease

00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:52.170
in false positive
rate, which is great,

00:19:52.170 --> 00:19:55.710
at least relative to each other.

00:19:55.710 --> 00:19:58.320
We need to actually
go back a few levels

00:19:58.320 --> 00:20:00.540
to look at the per
group performance

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:05.130
metrics on the absolute basis
to see what happened there.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:07.010
And when we do that
unfortunately we

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:11.030
see that most of the gains
and fairness that we made

00:20:11.030 --> 00:20:13.580
in that previous
plot are actually

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:18.540
due to the error rate mostly
increasing for the groups,

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:21.560
but increasing for the
other groups more than

00:20:21.560 --> 00:20:23.840
it did for the
African-American group.

00:20:23.840 --> 00:20:27.440
So basically what happened
here is the model said, OK, you

00:20:27.440 --> 00:20:31.790
want to make it more fair across
groups for false positive rate,

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:35.120
we'll make it so that the
other groups have more error

00:20:35.120 --> 00:20:38.090
and make it so that the
African-American group,

00:20:38.090 --> 00:20:39.890
their error relative
to the other groups

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:41.870
is lower than it was originally.

00:20:41.870 --> 00:20:45.990
Again, if fairness is our
goal here, that's fine.

00:20:45.990 --> 00:20:48.290
That's not a bad result.
However, it clearly

00:20:48.290 --> 00:20:51.180
did come with the tradeoff.

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:52.930
And whenever we
use these methods,

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:56.530
this is just something
we'll have to consider.

00:20:56.530 --> 00:20:58.690
Lastly, for fairness
in outcomes,

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:01.930
we want to consider
the possibility

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:03.740
of algorithmic recourse.

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:06.580
So if our models are making
decisions about people,

00:21:06.580 --> 00:21:09.400
we don't want them
to lose the ability

00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:12.160
to actually have some
influence over that decision.

00:21:12.160 --> 00:21:13.900
They should be
able to appeal it.

00:21:13.900 --> 00:21:17.140
They should be able to ask for
explanations as to why they

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:20.830
were given that decision, and
they should be given, if asked

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:23.050
or if they choose to ask,
recommendations for what

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:24.800
they can do to change it.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:26.420
For more info on
this I recommend

00:21:26.420 --> 00:21:29.660
looking at the work of
Ruha Benjamin, William

00:21:29.660 --> 00:21:31.295
Isaac, or Shakir Mohamed.

00:21:31.295 --> 00:21:33.170
Google any of those
names, and you'll come up

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:36.030
with a lot of good stuff.

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:39.200
And at that point, you
had fairness in outcomes,

00:21:39.200 --> 00:21:42.320
you had all three of the
main notions of fairness that

00:21:42.320 --> 00:21:44.120
have come up across
the research,

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:48.800
and you're doing a solid job
at doing data science more

00:21:48.800 --> 00:21:50.910
responsibly.

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:55.680
And fundamentally that's great,
because doing data science

00:21:55.680 --> 00:21:59.110
in this way is the
right thing to do.

00:21:59.110 --> 00:22:01.170
There's a lot of good
moral justifications

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:03.570
for why we should do
our work with a greater

00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:06.930
conception of fairness and
consider factors outside

00:22:06.930 --> 00:22:08.940
of the code and the
data and the predictions

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:10.770
that we normally deal with.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:14.550
Maybe as a profession we should
start thinking of ourselves

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:17.970
not just as data scientists,
but also ethnographers,

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:20.460
sociologists, anthropologists.

00:22:20.460 --> 00:22:22.403
Not just thinking about
it though, actually

00:22:22.403 --> 00:22:23.820
getting that
knowledge, and that's

00:22:23.820 --> 00:22:25.810
something we should seek out.

00:22:25.810 --> 00:22:27.953
But beyond being right--

00:22:27.953 --> 00:22:29.870
or beyond being the
morally right thing to do,

00:22:29.870 --> 00:22:32.590
I also want to make
one more point.

00:22:32.590 --> 00:22:35.950
This is not just the
right thing to do.

00:22:35.950 --> 00:22:40.750
Doing these practices, and doing
data science more responsibly

00:22:40.750 --> 00:22:44.590
in this way is doing
better data science.

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:46.840
By doing responsible
data science

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:48.700
you'll build better
models, you'll

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:52.840
have less errors sneaking
through to model deployment,

00:22:52.840 --> 00:22:55.990
and fundamentally you'll
be serving all the people

00:22:55.990 --> 00:22:58.450
that you're already serving
much better than you

00:22:58.450 --> 00:23:02.230
are now and with a greater
eye to safety and fairness.

00:23:02.230 --> 00:23:07.340
And if that's not worth doing,
then I don't know what is.

00:23:07.340 --> 00:23:09.860
So what I have here
to wrap up with

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:12.980
are a few resources, additional
links that people can look at.

00:23:12.980 --> 00:23:15.950
But I have a number of
other resources in addition

00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:19.220
to code to generate those plots
and do a few other analyses

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:20.300
on the GitHub.

00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:22.920
So if you're interested,
please do check that out.

00:23:22.920 --> 00:23:25.780
But otherwise, thanks
for coming to the talk

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:27.460
and for sitting here with me.

00:23:27.460 --> 00:23:31.090
And hope to connect
with you soon

00:23:31.090 --> 00:23:34.150
and see you using these
techniques yourself.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:34.840
Best of luck.

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:45.390
[MUSIC PLAYING]

